    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm looking at some austin zoning stuff. there's this conflict between the residential setback requirements and some older encroachment clauses in the municipal code. if a building's foundation slightly overlaps a property line due to an old easement, how do people usually argue for a mixed-use reclassification without getting blocked by the encroachment rules?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When dealing with conflicts between residential setback requirements and old encroachment clauses, stakeholders typically need to gather historical evidence of the easement in question. This may involve commissioning a title search, consulting historical deeds, or acquiring a survey that references the easement. Once established, property owners can approach the city with the intent to reclassify the property for mixed-use, providing detailed plans that show the easement's historical validity and how it aligns with current urban planning needs. Coupled with a proposed comprehensive zoning plan that accommodates the encroachment, this evidence can help negotiate terms favorable to a reclassification notwithstanding the encroachment rules.",
