    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. but i'm worried about the actual enforcement side. if the city adjuster sees that encroachment as a \"violation\" rather than just a historical quirk, they might try to claim it's an unauthorized intrusion. how do we frame the argument so it doesn't look like we're just trying to sneak something into a space where it doesn't belong?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To preempt any enforcement issues, your argument should be rooted in the principle of 'grandfathering', a common legal concept wherein pre-existing conditions are exempt from new laws due to their establishment before such laws were enacted. Clearly document the history of the easement's existence, emphasizing its status as a prior right that has been relied upon. Additionally, consider commissioning a legal opinion that reinforces the property's lawful status given the historical easement. When presenting this to local authorities, demonstrate how the continued existence of the encroachment is consistent with its original intent, and that a mixed-use reclassification would enhance rather than diminish the neighborhood's character or property values. Offer to work with the city to establish a formal agreement that codifies the status of the easement and the proposed reclassification, thereby precluding any impression of unauthorized intrusion.",
